1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:06,507
In behavior labs, someone still watches every second of video and labels it by hand.

2
00:00:06,507 --> 00:00:11,472
For social behavior, that takes three to four times the video's length.

3
00:00:11,472 --> 00:00:16,438
This is Etho, for Push the Frontier: no training, just plain-English definitions.

4
00:00:16,438 --> 00:00:19,748
On a public benchmark, it reaches 0.58 F1.

5
00:00:20,300 --> 00:00:24,024
Behavior scoring sits underneath neuroscience research and drug-safety studies.

6
00:00:24,024 --> 00:00:31,886
Today it takes a person with a stopwatch, or software you must first feed hand-labeled examples of every behavior.

7
00:00:31,886 --> 00:00:34,369
Etho needs one sentence per behavior.

8
00:00:35,300 --> 00:00:39,438
This is a test video Etho was never tuned on.

9
00:00:39,438 --> 00:00:44,403
I paste the dataset authors' own definitions of attack, investigation and mount.

10
00:00:44,403 --> 00:00:46,886
Muse Spark decides what to track.

11
00:00:46,886 --> 00:00:52,679
SAM 3.1 follows both mice, and keeps them apart even when they're tangled together.

12
00:00:52,679 --> 00:01:01,783
Muse Spark turns each definition into a short Python rule, and Muse Spark Open double-checks the borderline moments from strips of frames.

13
00:01:04,300 --> 00:01:20,024
Around the models, built this week: a gateway that caps every dollar and caches every call, the tracking and identity code, a locked-down sandbox for the model-written rules, and the two-step check, with more than six hundred tests.

14
00:01:21,300 --> 00:01:23,783
Here's Etho beside the expert's labels.

15
00:01:23,783 --> 00:01:26,679
Every label is traceable, including the mistakes.

16
00:01:26,679 --> 00:01:30,817
At thirty-eight seconds, Etho says mount; the expert says attack.

17
00:01:30,817 --> 00:01:40,748
The frame check actually answered "attack", but a rule in the cascade set it aside, and the trace shows exactly which step did it.

18
00:01:40,748 --> 00:01:42,817
No label goes unexplained.

19
00:01:44,300 --> 00:01:44,714
Disagree?

20
00:01:44,714 --> 00:01:46,369
Mark a few bouts.

21
00:01:46,369 --> 00:01:50,507
Etho rewrites the rule and shows you exactly what changed.

22
00:01:50,507 --> 00:01:52,576
The researcher stays in control.

23
00:01:55,300 --> 00:02:00,679
On the nineteen official test videos, scored once: 0.58 F1, with no training.

24
00:02:00,679 --> 00:02:06,472
Supervised methods, trained on labeled data and given pose keypoints, reach 0.79 to 0.86.

25
00:02:06,472 --> 00:02:14,748
Etho works from raw video, unattended, in about the video's own length, for about eleven dollars per hour of footage.

26
00:02:14,748 --> 00:02:25,921
Our biggest surprise: Muse Spark Open, looking at six frames at low effort, added 0.2 F1, the biggest gain of any step on top of the rules.

27
00:02:27,300 --> 00:02:32,679
Same pipeline, new animal: fruit flies, from a one-sentence definition, with no retraining.

28
00:02:32,679 --> 00:02:39,300
It's a preview: it hasn't cleared the confidence bar we set, so there's no number here.

29
00:02:41,300 --> 00:02:46,679
Etho runs on Meta's models: SAM 3.1, Muse Spark and Muse Spark Open.

30
00:02:46,679 --> 00:02:50,817
It's for every lab that still scores behavior by hand.

31
00:02:50,817 --> 00:02:52,059
It's open source.

32
00:02:52,059 --> 00:02:54,955
Try it live at the link below.
